00:00:08.074 --> 00:00:09.342
And on Friday,

00:00:09.342 --> 00:00:10.844
they sent an email out to their customers

00:00:10.844 --> 00:00:12.312
to put up this ad,

00:00:12.312 --> 00:00:13.046
put it up right there

00:00:13.046 --> 00:00:14.748
on their website, it said.

00:00:14.748 --> 00:00:16.282
When you bank

00:00:16.282 --> 00:00:16.783
when your bank

00:00:16.783 --> 00:00:20.553
collapse, run, don't walk right to say

00:00:20.553 --> 00:00:22.622
40% at camp,

00:00:22.622 --> 00:00:23.690
the caption say,

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:25.825
I never liked the Bay Area.

00:00:25.825 --> 00:00:27.727
And could this happen?

00:00:27.727 --> 00:00:29.129
How could this happen?

00:00:29.129 --> 00:00:30.897
This all sucks.

00:00:30.897 --> 00:00:33.466
Promo code bank run.

00:00:33.466 --> 00:00:34.601
Is all right.

00:00:34.601 --> 00:00:36.536
So, Poppy, we're

00:00:36.536 --> 00:00:37.637
you know, we're talking about kids

00:00:37.637 --> 00:00:38.204
toys here.

00:00:38.204 --> 00:00:39.372
And you're live at the camp store

00:00:39.372 --> 00:00:40.440
right now.

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:41.574
Poppy, what are you seeing?

00:00:41.574 --> 00:00:42.575
Hello.

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:44.310
Hey, guys.

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:44.911
Good morning.

00:00:44.911 --> 00:00:45.812
We're with Ben

00:00:45.812 --> 00:00:48.448
Kaufman, the founder of Camp and the CEO.

00:00:48.481 --> 00:00:51.017
This is your idea over the weekend.

00:00:51.017 --> 00:00:51.951
And we're smiling

00:00:51.951 --> 00:00:53.253
because thank goodness

00:00:53.253 --> 00:00:55.488
your deposits are now assured

00:00:55.488 --> 00:00:56.523
because of the federal government.

00:00:56.523 --> 00:00:57.657
But this was

00:00:57.657 --> 00:00:59.759
a last ditch effort over the weekend

00:00:59.759 --> 00:01:01.261
to try not to collapse.

00:01:01.261 --> 00:01:03.296
That's how dependent you guys were

00:01:03.863 --> 00:01:05.165
on SDB.

00:01:05.165 --> 00:01:07.000
Yeah.
About 85% of our company's

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:09.469
cash assets were at 85%.

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:12.305
Millions of dollars in stock.

00:01:12.305 --> 00:01:12.739
Okay.

00:01:12.739 --> 00:01:15.508
So what happened to you Friday?

00:01:16.142 --> 00:01:16.843
So on Friday,

00:01:16.843 --> 00:01:18.211
when we got the alert

00:01:18.211 --> 00:01:19.779
that the FDIC was

00:01:19.779 --> 00:01:21.114
was taking control of the bank,

00:01:21.114 --> 00:01:22.082
we had no idea

00:01:22.082 --> 00:01:23.683
what would be the next step.

00:01:23.683 --> 00:01:24.517
We did not know

00:01:24.517 --> 00:01:25.618
how long it was going to take

00:01:25.618 --> 00:01:27.353
for us to get our cash out.

00:01:27.353 --> 00:01:28.354
We still kind of don't they say

00:01:28.354 --> 00:01:29.689
today, we'll see. We'll see what happens.

00:01:29.689 --> 00:01:31.191
You don't know if you can get your money.

00:01:31.191 --> 00:01:32.358
What we hope we hope we can.

00:01:32.358 --> 00:01:33.393
And we're so grateful

00:01:33.393 --> 00:01:34.461
that the Fed stepped

00:01:34.461 --> 00:01:35.295
in in the way they did.

00:01:36.563 --> 00:01:37.764
But we

00:01:37.764 --> 00:01:39.432
did what startups do, which is we

00:01:39.432 --> 00:01:41.101
we kind of took matters into our own hand

00:01:41.101 --> 00:01:42.302
and we kind of had that fight

00:01:42.302 --> 00:01:43.837
for survival.

00:01:43.837 --> 00:01:45.138
It's a similar fight for survival.

00:01:45.138 --> 00:01:45.572
We felt

00:01:45.572 --> 00:01:46.773
when when COVID happened

00:01:46.773 --> 00:01:49.309
and we ran an experiment through a store.

00:01:49.309 --> 00:01:51.911
And so we turn to our most loyal group

00:01:51.911 --> 00:01:54.013
of customers and said, hey, we need

00:01:54.013 --> 00:01:55.515
we need inflows of cash right now.

00:01:55.515 --> 00:01:56.616
We set up a new account

00:01:56.616 --> 00:01:58.284
and kind of pushed our weekend sales

00:01:58.284 --> 00:01:59.419
to a new account.

00:01:59.419 --> 00:02:01.521
JP Morgan Chase. JP Morgan Chase.

00:02:01.521 --> 00:02:02.922
And we said we need your help.

00:02:02.922 --> 00:02:04.124
And the outpouring of support

00:02:04.124 --> 00:02:06.326
was so absolutely critical

00:02:06.326 --> 00:02:07.260
to all our viewers.

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:08.628
And guys back in the studio.

00:02:08.628 --> 00:02:10.196
The reason that I wanted to have been on

00:02:10.196 --> 00:02:11.898
is I'm one of those loyal customers.

00:02:11.898 --> 00:02:12.699
I got little kids.

00:02:12.699 --> 00:02:13.800
You've got three little kids.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.902
I get this email Saturday,

00:02:15.902 --> 00:02:17.370
Friday afternoon, and I'm like,

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:19.672
wow, they are turning to

00:02:19.672 --> 00:02:20.940
everyone who's a customer

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:22.242
to try to save the business.

00:02:22.242 --> 00:02:24.310
That's how dire the streets were for you.

00:02:24.644 --> 00:02:25.645
Yeah, we had to pretend

00:02:25.645 --> 00:02:26.880
like it was the end of times.

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:28.915
And not pretended it felt that way. Yes.

00:02:29.349 --> 00:02:31.017
And we had to find a way to

00:02:31.017 --> 00:02:32.719
to fight for survival.

00:02:32.719 --> 00:02:33.353
And that was, again,

00:02:33.353 --> 00:02:34.254
turning to customers,

00:02:34.254 --> 00:02:34.721
providing them

00:02:34.721 --> 00:02:36.156
with a little bit of offer. Right.

00:02:36.156 --> 00:02:36.489
There was

00:02:36.489 --> 00:02:37.690
there was value in it for customers.

00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:40.226
We offered 40% off, and we were

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:41.928
we were so lucky to have so much

00:02:41.928 --> 00:02:42.662
amazing support

00:02:42.662 --> 00:02:43.563
in all the things

00:02:43.563 --> 00:02:44.631
that keep you up at night.

00:02:44.631 --> 00:02:47.100
As the founder of a business,

00:02:47.133 --> 00:02:49.502
small business of as a startup.

00:02:49.502 --> 00:02:52.172
Did you ever think that your deposits

00:02:52.438 --> 00:02:54.240
that were largely held in, you know,

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:56.676
securities, treasuries, bonds,

00:02:56.676 --> 00:02:58.778
would not be available?

00:02:58.778 --> 00:02:59.746
No.
I mean, like you

00:02:59.746 --> 00:03:01.147
said, like running a startup as hard,

00:03:01.147 --> 00:03:02.749
running a retailer as hard

00:03:02.749 --> 00:03:03.883
running a DTC product.

00:03:03.883 --> 00:03:05.518
Companies are running out of running

00:03:05.518 --> 00:03:07.353
an experiential environment. It's hard.

00:03:07.353 --> 00:03:08.621
We never had to think about

00:03:08.621 --> 00:03:10.890
like the safety of our bank.

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:12.759
That's an added layer. Okay.

00:03:12.759 --> 00:03:15.361
So I spoke to a lot of founders

00:03:15.361 --> 00:03:16.796
this weekend and

00:03:16.796 --> 00:03:18.031
another startup founder

00:03:18.031 --> 00:03:20.733
and CEO told me that it was a requirement

00:03:21.034 --> 00:03:23.536
that if you got lending from SVP,

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:25.805
you had to hold all of your cash

00:03:25.805 --> 00:03:26.739
deposit there.

00:03:26.739 --> 00:03:28.975
They required that. Is that true for you?

00:03:29.175 --> 00:03:30.510
It was true.

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:31.244
At a certain point

00:03:31.244 --> 00:03:32.812
in our company's history,

00:03:32.812 --> 00:03:34.514
we had a line of credit with SDB.

00:03:34.514 --> 00:03:36.282
We no longer did at the time.

00:03:36.282 --> 00:03:37.584
Of the collapse.

00:03:37.584 --> 00:03:39.085
But because all of you know,

00:03:39.085 --> 00:03:40.486
when you check out of the register,

00:03:40.486 --> 00:03:41.788
the money automatically went to CB.

00:03:41.788 --> 00:03:43.256
It was all kind of wired in.

00:03:43.256 --> 00:03:43.957
Yeah, just we

00:03:43.957 --> 00:03:45.825
we never actually transferred that away.

00:03:45.825 --> 00:03:49.128
Once that but that it would explain why

00:03:49.128 --> 00:03:52.398
so many founders and startups had all of

00:03:52.398 --> 00:03:54.133
their cash in this bank. Yes.

00:03:55.201 --> 00:03:55.735
Yeah.

00:03:55.835 --> 00:03:58.004
We there was it was bank to use.

00:03:58.004 --> 00:03:59.272
And they made you do that

00:03:59.272 --> 00:04:01.274
if you wanted to get credit from them.

00:04:01.274 --> 00:04:02.775
Yep. What do you do?

00:04:02.775 --> 00:04:04.611
Have a message for the management group

00:04:04.611 --> 00:04:06.779
or the CEO? The management of SVP.

00:04:06.779 --> 00:04:08.881
Do you think this is bad management?

00:04:08.881 --> 00:04:09.549
I don't know.

00:04:09.549 --> 00:04:11.884
I mean, I'm I'm I'm not a toy store.

00:04:12.018 --> 00:04:13.519
Yes. I'm not an economist.

00:04:13.519 --> 00:04:15.054
I don't know how to manage,

00:04:15.054 --> 00:04:17.457
you know, money at that scale.

00:04:17.457 --> 00:04:20.126
I, frankly, like SVP, was there for us

00:04:20.126 --> 00:04:20.893
when we needed them.

00:04:20.893 --> 00:04:22.295
They were there for other startups,

00:04:22.295 --> 00:04:23.096
so we needed them.

00:04:23.096 --> 00:04:24.731
And again, startups are hard.

00:04:24.731 --> 00:04:25.298
And is that why

00:04:25.298 --> 00:04:26.466
you didn't pull all your money out

00:04:26.466 --> 00:04:28.501
when you heard all this Twitter chatter

00:04:28.501 --> 00:04:30.003
and VC talking about pulling money out?

00:04:30.003 --> 00:04:31.304
We attempted to on Thursday.

00:04:31.304 --> 00:04:33.206
And you think it just didn't settle

00:04:33.206 --> 00:04:34.073
at the other bank.

00:04:34.073 --> 00:04:35.441
I have to think of all your employees

00:04:35.441 --> 00:04:36.576
first. What?

00:04:36.576 --> 00:04:38.144
So what were they saying to you?

00:04:38.144 --> 00:04:39.679
What were they feeling?

00:04:39.679 --> 00:04:40.413
Well, again,

00:04:40.413 --> 00:04:42.015
we were lucky to have an account

00:04:42.015 --> 00:04:44.284
already set up at JPMorgan Chase.

00:04:44.284 --> 00:04:45.952
I the first thing I said to our employees

00:04:45.952 --> 00:04:46.286
is that we have

00:04:46.286 --> 00:04:47.153
a little bit of cash there

00:04:47.153 --> 00:04:49.422
because if you pay in cash in our stores,

00:04:49.422 --> 00:04:50.723
you know, the store managers

00:04:50.723 --> 00:04:51.591
ran it over to Chase.

00:04:51.591 --> 00:04:52.258
We had a little bit of cash

00:04:52.258 --> 00:04:53.092
and we knew we were going to be able

00:04:53.092 --> 00:04:55.561
to make payroll this week.

00:04:55.561 --> 00:04:56.329
But we said, like,

00:04:56.329 --> 00:04:57.163
the future is uncertain,

00:04:57.163 --> 00:04:58.531
we don't know what the next step

00:04:58.531 --> 00:04:59.399
is going to be.

00:04:59.399 --> 00:05:00.033
And so we again,

00:05:00.033 --> 00:05:01.567
we turned to the turn to customers

00:05:01.567 --> 00:05:03.436
and immediately store managers

00:05:03.436 --> 00:05:04.337
as saying there was

00:05:04.337 --> 00:05:05.571
the stores were packed.

00:05:05.571 --> 00:05:06.472
And you know what

00:05:06.472 --> 00:05:06.906
we saw

00:05:06.906 --> 00:05:07.940
online from people

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:08.274
that were

00:05:08.274 --> 00:05:09.409
not only existing customers,

00:05:09.409 --> 00:05:10.743
but new customers wanting to support

00:05:10.743 --> 00:05:11.978
this brand was,

00:05:11.978 --> 00:05:12.979
you know, I'll never forget it.

00:05:12.979 --> 00:05:14.113
It's amazing.

00:05:14.113 --> 00:05:15.248
I'm so glad.

00:05:15.248 --> 00:05:17.250
Does it change finally how you

00:05:18.785 --> 00:05:20.453
hold your money going forward?

00:05:20.453 --> 00:05:21.587
Do you now split it up?

00:05:21.587 --> 00:05:24.123
I think a lot of heads of small companies

00:05:24.123 --> 00:05:25.458
and startups are thinking,

00:05:25.458 --> 00:05:27.060
do we put 20% in this bank

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:29.062
and 20% this bank and 20% in this bank.

00:05:29.562 --> 00:05:30.363
Yeah,
I think that's going

00:05:30.363 --> 00:05:31.364
to have to be a consideration

00:05:31.364 --> 00:05:32.498
moving forward.

00:05:32.832 --> 00:05:34.901
You know, I don't want to do this again.

00:05:34.901 --> 00:05:35.835
No, no one.

00:05:35.835 --> 00:05:36.836
We're glad you're okay.

00:05:36.836 --> 00:05:38.271
You're going to make your next payroll.

00:05:38.271 --> 00:05:38.871
We sure are.

00:05:38.871 --> 00:05:40.139
I mean, again, like we

00:05:40.139 --> 00:05:42.241
the support this weekend was incredible.

00:05:42.241 --> 00:05:42.875
We're just going to go back

00:05:42.875 --> 00:05:44.377
to the regular startup fights,

00:05:44.377 --> 00:05:45.311
you know, the regular,

00:05:45.311 --> 00:05:46.879
you know, fighting, fighting for survival

00:05:46.879 --> 00:05:48.147
that every startups do,

00:05:48.147 --> 00:05:49.182
even when they're banking stable.

00:05:49.182 --> 00:05:51.150
You made it through COVID,

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:52.652
you made it through this

00:05:52.652 --> 00:05:55.088
I told you we'd get all of our kids.

00:05:55.088 --> 00:05:55.488
Thank you.

00:05:55.488 --> 00:05:56.823
Christmas gifts, birthday gifts.

00:05:56.823 --> 00:05:58.324
You know, the staff is always so lovely.

00:05:58.324 --> 00:05:59.492
Good luck. Thank you so much.

00:05:59.492 --> 00:06:00.159
Very much. Extra.

00:06:00.159 --> 00:06:01.894
Ben Kaufman. Hey, guys, back to you.

00:06:01.894 --> 00:06:02.962
And one of the coolest

00:06:02.962 --> 00:06:04.297
old school rocking horses

00:06:05.431 --> 00:06:06.165
in that store,

00:06:06.165 --> 00:06:07.367
Poppy, as you know,

00:06:07.367 --> 00:06:09.469
they love the old school.

00:06:10.069 --> 00:06:11.070
It's one of my favorites.

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:12.004
It was great. I love that.

00:06:12.004 --> 00:06:14.374
He said I'm a toy store owner.

00:06:14.807 --> 00:06:15.274
Right.

00:06:15.274 --> 00:06:16.843
Not an economist and an economist.

00:06:16.843 --> 00:06:17.844
And I think that speaks

00:06:17.844 --> 00:06:19.245
to a lot of folks,

00:06:19.245 --> 00:06:20.513
what a lot of folks are dealing with.

